 seconds. Aurel's count was thirty-six the week before his last climb, so time is short. Aurel's watch runs fast, matching the bay. The lid shows scratched counts from 90 down to 31, then 'THE REST UNDER THE BELL'. There is one church bell, and every clock in town is set by it. The church door is always unlocked. Pike's padlock is on the tower door, and Mara holds the only key. The tower clock was set about ten minutes behind the gears' marks. The hatch is bolted from above and has never been opened by Mara. There is candle wax on the rungs. The wet rope has 32 knots, the last crusted with grey mud; it is a sounding line for the Throat, measuring 'how near she's come.' Wen: 'A warden's not a man, it's a chair.' The slow clock is 'a bridle.' The ebb leaves weed in rings centered on the Throat. After the bell rang, Pike's watch was two minutes slow, Aurel's watch had jumped ahead, and the tide clock was a minute off. Dawn of the climb: grey, with fog on the water.", "open_threads": "Who bolted the hatch, and are they still up there? Who has been sounding the Throat, and what is 'she'? Who holds the warden's chair now? What the slow clock bridles. What answered the bell, and what answered Tobin. What lies under the bell (ledgers, hop 21). What 'again' means. What happens when the pause runs out. What Tobin was doing at the tower before dawn.", "text": "Mara closed the watch and felt it ticking too quickly against her palm. \"I'm going back up at first light.\" Pike lowered his lantern. \"First light, then,\" he said. \"I'll bring the oil.\" That night Mara lay awake in her room above the harbor office. Beyond the window the bay was black and smooth. She waited for a ripple from the Throat, then counted on Aurel's watch until the next one came. Thirty-five. She inked it on her wrist beneath the others. When she went down for her coat, the pocket was empty. Tobin sat at the kitchen table in the dark with the key in his fist. \"He counted too,\" he said. \"Every night, right to the end.\" \"Then you know why I have to.\" \"I know what it cost.\" She held out her hand and waited. For a long time he didn't move. Then he set the key on the table and kept his fingers on it. \"Go up if you must. Read the clock. Look at the gears. But don't open the hatch.\" \"Why?\" \"Because he did.\" He lifted his hand and went upstairs without another word. Dawn came grey and low, with fog lying flat on the water. Pike stood by the church door, his lantern lit though the light had come, his breath steaming. \"Your father was here an hour ago,\" he said. \"Stood looking up at the tower. Didn't say a word to me.\" Mara looked up too. The tower's face was blurred by fog, but its hands had moved."}
