ck in town is set by it. The night Aurel drowned, the bell rang once by itself. The tower door is padlocked, and Mara holds the only key. The tower clock was six minutes behind and catching up. The bell chamber has open louvers, mud and black weed, a burned-down candle and a wet trail from the hatch to the louvers. There are three damp ledgers in oilcloth. In the first, each count is dated in Aurel's hand. Thirty-one is dated the night he drowned. Thirty and twenty-nine are undated. 'When it reaches nothing, she will be home' is torn into the paper. The second and third ledgers are unread. The wet rope has 32 knots and is a sounding line for the Throat. Wen: 'A warden's not a man, it's a chair.' The slow clock is 'a bridle.'", "open_threads": "What was in the bell chamber? How did the count fall from thirty-six to thirty-one in a week? Why are thirty and twenty-nine undated, and who wrote them? Why did Aurel lie about burning the ledgers? What rang the bell that night, and what did Tobin find at the top? Who is sounding the Throat? What is 'she'? Who holds the warden's chair now? What does the slow clock bridle? What answered Tobin? What happens at nothing? What Tobin was doing at the tower before dawn. What the other two ledgers hold.", "text": "\"That's not possible,\" Pike said. \"You told me it was thirty-six the week before.\" Thirty and twenty-nine had no dates at all. The door banged open. Tobin stood in the doorway, his coat soaked with fog, and his eyes went straight to the ledgers. The color left his face. \"Where did you get those?\" Mara didn't answer. He crossed the room in three strides and laid his hand flat on the open page. \"He told me he burned them,\" Tobin whispered. \"He stood on the quay and swore it.\" \"Then why is thirty-one dated the night he died?\" Mara asked. \"You were there. Why would he lie to you?\" Tobin took his hand away. \"I don't know why he lied.\" \"Then tell me what you do know.\" He looked at the clock, not at her. \"I climbed the tower that night, after he took the boat out. The bell rang once, and nobody was on the rope.\" His voice thinned. \"I went up to stop it. That's all.\" \"That isn't all.\" \"It's all I'll say.\" Behind him the brass clock gave a sharp click. Both hands leapt forward a full quarter hour in a single stroke. Pike swore and fumbled out his pocket watch. Outside a gull screamed, and under it came a low hiss along the quay. Mara went to the window. The tide was coming in hours early. Black water slid over the stones and was already lapping at the harbor office step."}
