ed trails toward the Throat).", "facts": "Gullhaven sits on a horseshoe bay. The Throat is the narrow mouth of the bay. The brass tide clock hangs in the harbor office, and its drift is accelerating. The tide floods early and leaves black weed. The pause is 32 seconds and is falling about one a day. There is one church bell, and all the clocks are set by it. It rang by itself, thirty-two times, the night Aurel died, and again in hop 28. It hummed in hops 34 and 37. The tower is padlocked, and Mara holds the only key. A sea passage with iron rungs leads up to a round room with a carved seat (now empty, warm, wet ring), then to the bell chamber. A black weed trail runs toward the Throat. Ledger 2: 'The chair must be kept warm, or she comes up to sit in it herself.' The rope has 32 knots and is a Throat sounding line. A warden is 'a chair'. The slow clock is 'a bridle'. The doctor called Aurel 'drowned' though he had no sea water on him. Slow rings 32 seconds apart rise and spread toward the Throat. At the end of hop 40 the Merrow turned for harbor a little after two in the morning.", "open_threads": "Who or what is the figure? Did it take the coins? Who wrote the page in Tobin's hand? What is 'she'? Why did the hum use Aurel's voice while he lived? Why does the count of 32 recur? What makes the rings? How did Aurel drown dry? What told Tobin the count three weeks ago? Who sounds the Throat? What does the slow clock bridle? What happens at nothing?", "text": "Tobin had not moved. \"I didn't run,\" he said. \"I stayed, because Mother said to wait for the tally. When the candle burned down, the hum stopped all at once, the way a man stops talking when someone walks in. And the bell began to ring by itself. Thirty-two times. I counted.\" He stared at the black water. \"I climbed down and ran home along the shore. From the point I could see Father's lantern out by the Throat. It went out while I watched. They found the Gannet at first light, drifting inside the Throat with her oars shipped neatly, as if he'd set them down to listen. He lay in the bottom of her, dry as a Sunday. Drowned, the doctor said, with not a drop of sea on him.\" Mara's hand closed on the watch in her pocket. \"Mother was waiting at the door,\" Tobin went on. \"She didn't ask about Father. She asked if the chair was warm. I said yes. She burned my candle stub, wrote the number in the ledger, and never said his name again.\" \"And three weeks ago?\" Pike asked. \"What told you?\" Tobin shook his head. \"Not tonight.\" Another ring rose off the bow, wider than the last, and slid toward the narrows where the Gannet had drifted. Mara counted. Thirty-two. Pike held the tiller loose. \"We follow those and find what's making them,\" he said, \"or we go home and wait for it to come to us.\" He looked at Mara, not Tobin. She looked down at the ledgers in her lap, at her grandfather's careful numbers. \"Home,\" she said. \"I need the clock and his counts. Then we come back.\" Pike put the Merrow about."}
