 a dragging hem; carried a candle; hums a count; left weed trails toward the Throat).", "facts": "Gullhaven sits on a horseshoe bay. The Throat is the narrow mouth of the bay. The brass tide clock hangs in the harbor office, and its drift is accelerating. The tide floods early and leaves black weed, now heaped on the shore path to the point. The pause is 32 seconds and is falling about one a day. Aurel's ledger shows the same pattern: counts falling about one a day, sometimes steady for one day but never two, ending at 32 beside Ines's note of the date he died. Mara reckons about 32 days to nothing. There is one church bell, and all the clocks are set by it. It rang by itself thirty-two times the night Aurel died, and again in hop 28. It hummed in hops 34 and 37. The tower is padlocked, and Mara holds the only key. A sea passage with iron rungs leads up to a round room with a carved seat, then to the bell chamber. Per Wen, the passage also continues down from beneath the tower. The seat was warm with a wet ring when Mara saw it, but cold when Tobin went up three weeks ago; he says it had always been warm before. Ledger 2 reads: 'The chair must be kept warm, or she comes up to sit in it herself.' The rope has 32 knots and is a Throat sounding line. A warden is 'a chair'. The slow clock is 'a bridle'. Slow rings 32 seconds apart spread toward the Throat and faded before the harbor wall. Wen's riddles: the chair has stood 'empty but not unsat' for thirty years; 'something keeps a seat warm that has no warmth of its own'; the sitter 'went down to be counted' and 'came back up to finish the count'; its warmth is borrowed, and no man in Gullhaven lent it or could; 'ask your rope, it knows how deep.'", "open_threads": "Who or what is the figure, and is it the sitter? Did it take the coins? Who wrote the page in Tobin's hand? What is 'she'? Why did the hum use Aurel's voice? Why did Aurel's count stop at 32, the same as now? What makes the rings? How did Aurel drown dry? Where did the sitter go three weeks ago, and what counted it? Who sounds the Throat? What does the slow clock bridle? What happens at nothing? Where does the stair below the tower lead? What do the rope's knots measure?", "text": "Old Wen's cottage crouched on the point with its door already open. He stood in it, leaning on his cane. \"Three come walking where the water walked,\" he said. \"One brings a watch that stopped, one brings a boat that floats, and one brings coins that buy nothing.\" Tobin's hand closed in his pocket. \"We came about the chair,\" Mara said. Wen smiled with half his teeth. \"Yours has stood empty thirty years, girl, since the old man went out dry. Empty, but not unsat. Something keeps a seat warm that has no warmth of its own.\" He stepped back and let them into a room that smelled of tar and old salt. A diver's helmet sat on a shelf, green at the seams. Tobin stayed by the door. \"It was cold,\" he said. \"Three weeks ago. It was never cold before.\" Wen nodded as if Tobin had answered a question. \"A sitter may rise when it's called, harbormaster. It went down to be counted, and it came back up to finish the count. Its warmth is borrowed. No man in Gullhaven lent it, and none could.\" Mara set the lantern on the table. \"You told Pike there's a way in from below. Where?\" Wen lowered himself into his chair by the cold hearth. \"When I was young we went under the tower for the bishop's silver. Never found it. Found a stair instead, and that stair goes down farther than it goes up.\" He tapped his cane twice on the boards. \"Ask your rope, girl. It knows how deep.\" Pike looked at the coil on Mara's shoulder and said nothing."}
