 a long moment nothing came back but the drip of the walls. Then a woman's voice, rough from shouting over wind. Sal Henney, who mended nets on the quay. \"How long's a turn?\" \"I don't know,\" Mara called. \"Shorter, the more of us there are.\" Someone laughed, high and frightened, and stopped. Then Dell cleared his throat. \"My shop's half under already,\" he said. \"I'd sooner sit than bail.\" A single pair of boots struck the top stair. Lantern light swung down the curve of the wall, yellow and unsteady. Behind the chair, the cold gathered itself, and for the first time since Mara had spoken of turns, the voice answered. \"He is not counted.\" Water lay still on the third stair. Far above, Wen called, \"Nineteen.\""}
