Well. Damn,' and set it back down. The first Quell still holds his hand. Dell (chandler; his shop is flooding) is holding Ines. Sal Henney (net-mender, wiry, swears freely). The townsfolk hold the stair as a chain of lanterns, humming. The first Quell is the voice (tired, patient; 'does not unsay'). She is a shape of wet stone, weed and long hair, standing upright and taller than Pike. Her voice is hoarse and near. She had counted tides, the drowned, coins and years, but no one had ever counted her.", "facts": "Gullhaven sits on a horseshoe bay, and the Throat is its mouth. Zero has been called. The chair has never been empty, because hands stayed down. It is unoccupied but kept warm. One that keeps is kept. Three that keep are not held: any one of the three may lift a hand as long as the others stay down. If the chair is empty even for a breath, the sea fills it. There is one church bell, and voices on the stair make it hum. Beneath the tower, a stone stair leads to a round, weeping room holding the twin chair. Ledger 2: 'The chair must be kept warm, or she comes up to sit in it herself.' Rule of three: one alone, it keeps; with two, it takes one; with three, it counts and lets pass. Coins are fare back. Coins, Dell's copper, Sal's needle and Ines's ring lie on the left arm. A living hand warms the sitter. The stone behind the chair keeps a hollow of the first Quell's shape. The sea has drawn back past the sixth step. The first Quell's dripping water does not chill the chair.", "open_threads": "Why is Ines growing heavier? (Her keeping stretched her across several places, and she is gathering back into one body.) Does the first Quell let go of Pike and step away? Who keeps the chair afterward, perhaps the town in threes by turns? Does the tide return to its proper time?", "text": "The chair beneath her lay open, its seat dark and bare, and Mara felt the cold of the room lean toward it. Between their three hands, the empty seat stayed warm, and the cold found nothing to fill. The first Quell looked at the three of them, one after another, slowly, the way a person counts. \"Three,\" she said. Her voice was in the room now, hoarse and near. On the stair, the sea slid back past the sixth step. In the tower, Ines coughed, and Dell laughed in surprise. The first Quell turned Pike's hand over in hers, as if reading it. \"I counted the tides,\" she said. \"I counted the drowned, and the coins, and the years. No one ever counted me.\" She touched Tobin's shoulder, then the back of Mara's wrist, where the inked numbers ran. \"One keeps, and is kept. I was kept a long time.\" Water dripped from her hair onto the chair arm and did not chill it. \"Three that keep are not held. Any one of you may lift a hand, so long as the others stay.\" Mara stared at her. \"Show me.\" \"Lift,\" the first Quell said to Pike. Pike looked at Mara, and she nodded. Slowly he raised his palm a finger's width off the wood. The cold leaned in, found Mara's hand and Tobin's still down, and leaned away again. The seat stayed warm. Pike breathed out through his beard. \"Well,\" he said. \"Damn.\" He set his hand back down. Then Tobin lifted his, and again the chair held."}
