r, cracked with relief. \"It's going back. It's below the fifth step.\" Tobin looked over his shoulder at Bram. \"Your turn, if you'll have it.\" Bram waded over and studied the chair the way he might study a barrel stave. Then he set his broad hand beside Tobin's. \"Ready.\" Tobin lifted. The cold nosed at the gap and found nothing, because Bram's hand was already there. Tobin rose stiffly out of the water, knees cracking, and stared at his palm as if it belonged to someone else. Nessa's lantern shook. \"What if I do it wrong?\" \"There's no wrong,\" Mara said. \"Put yours over mine. I'll slide out when you're down.\" Nessa laid her small cold hand on Mara's knuckles. Mara drew her own hand away beneath it, and Nessa's palm settled flat on the stone. The chair held. Sal, Bram and Nessa kept it now, and not a Quell among them. Mara stepped back beside her father and Pike. For the first time in her life, no one in her family was keeping anything. The first Quell watched the three hands and let out a long breath, like surf drawing through shingle. \"Below the fourth step!\" Dell called. Then, sharper: \"She's moving. Ines is moving.\""}
