r by Aurel's watch; no one sits alone and no one is held. The walls are dry. It is now dawn. The morning tide came in at its proper time and the town noticed. The brass clock is only a hair ahead of Aurel's watch, nearly agreeing.", "open_threads": "Do the clock and watch agree exactly? How does the town settle into daytime turns? Ines teaching someone (Nessa) to cross sevens. A clean final image for hop 100.", "text": "Wen chalked a line under the first name and wrote Sal's beside it in small square letters. The brass clock stood three and a half minutes ahead. Mara did not move from it. The night went on in turns. At the next hour Bram went down, slow on every step, and the second Ashby twin came up stiff and grinning. Wen chalked it. Three minutes. At the hour after, Nessa went down with her chin high, and Rook came up rubbing his cold hands. Two minutes. Mara only watched. Toward dawn the windows grayed. Pike stood at the sill, looking out at the Throat. 'There,' he said. The sea came in across the flats, unhurried, wrinkling silver over the sand bars. Mara looked at the watch, then at the dial. The brass hand stood a hair ahead, no more than the width of a breath. Below, doors opened along the harbor. Someone on the quay shouted the hour, and someone else laughed. The bell hummed on. In the harbormaster's house Tobin set a bowl of broth on the table. Ines sat by the stove wrapped in a blanket, her sea-glass eyes on the window. She held the spoon in both hands and drank. 'Tide's right,' she said. Her voice was still rough, but it carried. 'Is it, Mam?' 'I'd know.' She ate again, then nodded at his ledger, open on the table. 'You still cross your sevens.' 'You taught me.' 'Good,' Ines said, and almost smiled."}
