ownsfolk on the stair.", "facts": "The rule of turns endures: three keep while the bell hums, hands change one at a time, no one sits alone and no one is held. The clock and the watch agree exactly. The tide is on time.", "open_threads": "None. The story is finished.", "text": "At the hour Nessa came down from the round room, flexing her stiff fingers, and Pike sent her to the harbormaster's house to eat. Ines laid a cold hand over the girl's and drew a short bar through a seven. 'So nobody takes it for a one. Counts matter here.' The door opened and Mara came in, salt in her hair, Aurel's watch in her fist. 'Wen has the clock,' she said. 'I wanted to see.' Ines lifted her chin toward the chair beside her. 'Then sit,' she said. 'Nobody sits alone.' Mara sat. Tobin poured her broth, and for a while nobody said anything. Nessa crossed her sevens. Ines watched the window. The stove ticked as it cooled. When the light had moved across the table, Mara kissed her grandmother's cold forehead and climbed back up the hill to the tower. Wen sat on his stool beneath the brass clock, chalk dust on his good fingers. On the stair below, the humming rose and settled. At the turn-change Mara opened Aurel's watch and held it beside the clock face. The second hands swept together. The minute hands lay one over the other. For the first time in thirty years, they agreed exactly. She uncapped her pen, looked at the inked numbers on her wrist, and then closed the watch instead. Wen chalked the next name. Pike called it up the stair. The bell hummed. Below the tower the tide stood where it should, flat and bright in the horseshoe of the bay, and the boats rocked gently at their lines, waiting for morning."}
